TIP : google "how to handle whitespaced files in linux".

The same concept of relative path can be used to solve this level 
but simply 'cat ./--spaces in this filename--' won't work here. 

There are multiple solutions to this level which are:
I:
    cat ./"spaces in this filename--" or cat "./spaces in this filename--"
II:
    cat ./--(hit tab it will auto-fill in the right format (i.e III))
III:
    cat ./--spaces\ in\ this\ filename--

Any of these commands will yeild the password to this level
Thank you.
